statement.p:
	  For each of the following linear systems, 
	  use Gaussian elimination to describe the solutions to the
	  following systems of linear equations.  In particular,
	  determine whether each linear system has exactly one
	  solution, infinitely many solutions, or no solutions.





	    \begin{alignedat}{4}
	    x \amp  {}+{}  \amp y \amp  {}+{}  \amp 2z \amp  {}={}  \amp 1 \\
	    2x \amp  {}-{}  \amp y \amp  {}-{}  \amp 2z \amp  {}={}  \amp 2 \\
	    -x \amp  {}+{}  \amp y \amp  {}+{}  \amp z \amp  {}={}
            \amp 0 \\ \\
	    \end{alignedat}







	    \begin{alignedat}{4}
	    -x \amp  {}-{}  \amp 2y \amp  {}+{}  \amp 2z \amp  {}={}  \amp -1 \\
	    2x \amp  {}+{}  \amp 4y \amp  {}-{}  \amp z \amp  {}={}  \amp 5 \\
	    x \amp  {}+{}  \amp 2y \amp  \amp  \amp  {}={}  \amp 3 \\ \\
	    \end{alignedat}







	    \begin{alignedat}{4}
	    -x \amp  {}-{}  \amp 2y \amp  {}+{}  \amp 2z \amp  {}={}  \amp -1 \\
	    2x \amp  {}+{}  \amp 4y \amp  {}-{}  \amp z \amp  {}={}  \amp 5 \\
	    x \amp  {}+{}  \amp 2y \amp  \amp  \amp  {}={}  \amp 2 \\ \\
	    \end{alignedat}

li.p:
Our aim is to apply a sequence of scaling, interchange, and replacement operations to first put the system into a triangular form. We begin by multiplying the first equation by -2 and adding it to the second equation. Next, we add the first equation to the third. This leads us to:
\begin{alignedat}{4} x \amp {}+{} \amp y \amp {}+{} \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \amp \amp -3y \amp {}-{} \amp 6z \amp {}={} \amp 0 \\ \amp \amp 2y \amp {}+{} \amp 3z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \end{alignedat}
We will now apply a scaling operation to make the coefficient of y equal 1 in the second equation.
\begin{alignedat}{4} x \amp {}+{} \amp y \amp {}+{} \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \amp \amp y \amp {}+{} \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp 0 \\ \amp \amp 2y \amp {}+{} \amp 3z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \end{alignedat}
Another replacement operation brings the system into a triangular form.
\begin{alignedat}{4} x \amp {}+{} \amp y \amp {}+{} \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \amp \amp y \amp {}+{} \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp 0 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp -z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \end{alignedat}

From here, we begin the process of back substitution seeking a decoupled system.
\begin{alignedat}{4} x \amp {}+{} \amp y \amp \amp \amp {}={} \amp 3 \\ \amp \amp y \amp \amp \amp {}={} \amp 2 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp z \amp {}={} \amp -1 \\ \end{alignedat}
Finally, we have the decoupled system
\begin{alignedat}{4} x \amp \amp \amp \amp \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \amp \amp y \amp \amp \amp {}={} \amp 2 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp z \amp {}={} \amp -1, \\ \end{alignedat}
which tells us that the solution space consists of the single solution (1,2,-1).

Once again, we begin with a sequence of replacement and scaling operations that lead to the triangular system
\begin{alignedat}{4} -x \amp {}-{} \amp 2y \amp {}+{} \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp -1 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp 0 \amp {}={} \amp 0 \\ \end{alignedat}
Back substitution gives us the system
\begin{alignedat}{4} x \amp {}+{} \amp 2y \amp \amp \amp {}={} \amp 3 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp 0 \amp {}={} \amp 0 \\ \end{alignedat}
The third equation does not impose a restriction on the solutions since it is satisfied for any (x,y,z). The second equation tells us that z must equal 1; however, there are infinitely many solutions to the first equation that have z=1. Therefore, this system has infinitely many solutions.

After applying two replacement and one scaling operation, we find
\begin{alignedat}{4} -x \amp {}-{} \amp 2y \amp {}+{} \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp -1 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \end{alignedat}
Another replacement operation leads to the system
\begin{alignedat}{4} -x \amp {}-{} \amp 2y \amp {}+{} \amp 2z \amp {}={} \amp -1 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp z \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \amp \amp \amp \amp 0 \amp {}={} \amp 1 \\ \end{alignedat}
Since the third equation has no solutions, the original system can have no solutions as well.
